WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.599
- Hi, I'm Mark Ruffalo, and
this is how I became Rob Bilott.

00:00:04.599 --> 00:00:07.010
(upbeat music)

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:09.690
Becoming Rob Bilott was appealing to me

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:14.200
because he was an atypical hero.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:18.990
He's the guy who is part of
a corporate defense company

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:23.690
that decides to take
on a case for a farmer

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:27.490
that he knew while he was
growing up against DuPont,

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:30.278
the biggest chemical company in the world.

00:00:30.278 --> 00:00:35.278
And he ends up uncovering
the biggest contamination

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:42.010
and poisoning of the world
from this one little case.

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:46.420
DuPont is knowingly poisoning
70,000 local residents

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.020
for the last 40 years.

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:50.290
My first impression of Rob Bilott

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:55.290
was somebody that was
very modest, soft-spoken,

00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:00.900
thoughtful, and very gentle,
something very sweet in him,

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:07.010
even though he was taking
on this corporate giant,

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.352
which made it a really
interesting kind of character,

00:01:11.352 --> 00:01:14.770
and not what you would
think of as somebody

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:19.620
who tackles something as
enormous as he does in this film.

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.320
I spent days with Rob Bilott.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.980
One of our first meetings,
I was meeting him

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:27.700
at the Taft Building in Cincinnati.

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.720
We were meeting at a particular time,

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:31.840
and I showed up a little bit early,

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:36.840
and I got to observe
him for maybe 10 minutes

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:40.473
without him knowing that I was there.

00:01:41.914 --> 00:01:43.410
I watched him, I actually followed him.

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:44.600
He was walking around,

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.860
and I was just regarding the way

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.683
he walked and the way he moved.

00:01:50.391 --> 00:01:52.770
He was on set with me all the time.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:55.690
I would call him all hours of the night

00:01:55.690 --> 00:02:00.010
to ask him things about
like what kind of snacks

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:04.240
did you eat during this, what
did you put in your coffee,

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:09.080
to why, why would you continue
on something so difficult

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:11.930
and lonely and alienating for so long?

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:13.970
But there was a lot of
questions that I had

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:17.340
about motivation because
this guy, what he did,

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:22.040
was so antithetical to
what most of us think

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:25.440
it is to be successful in America.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.070
Here he was in this
giant important law firm

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:32.240
that defended the biggest
companies in the world,

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.740
and he was working on
behalf of a cow farmer

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:39.720
in West Virginia who had
a 12th grade education.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:44.720
And he completely left the
American ideal of success

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:51.630
to go down this other
more just and, I'd say,

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:57.400
although he's not openly
spiritual or talks about spirit,

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:01.593
was a spiritual path for righteousness.

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:04.480
I wanted to know why.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:06.223
What compels somebody to do that?

00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:09.940
I don't know that I ever
really got to the bottom

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:12.760
of that question, other
than there's just something.

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:15.990
He had a greater belief in justice

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:19.170
than he did in things, in material.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:22.420
These companies, they have
all the money, all the time,

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:23.830
and they'll use it, trust me.

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:25.563
I know, I was one of them.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:28.520
- [Farmer] You're still one of 'em.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:30.704
- There's a scene that was
particularly hard for me

00:03:30.704 --> 00:03:35.600
and challenging was a scene
when Rob has the breakdown.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:40.140
His physical body just sort of breaks down

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:41.300
underneath the pressure.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.920
I don't understand that.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:45.830
I've never experienced anything like that,

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:50.830
so to make that work and
to have it be believable

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:54.610
was something that I was
struggling with a lot,

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:56.680
and I had a hard time shooting.

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:00.460
I probably shot that probably six times.

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:02.330
Then there's a couple scenes,

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:05.620
there's probably a six-page monologue

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:11.470
where he basically lays
out the entire history

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:15.820
of Teflon and C8 and
that was challenging too.

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:19.040
To be able to tell that
story and to do it in a way

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.720
that has the same kind of
thoughtfulness that Rob has,

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:25.850
and not as an actor try to go

00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:30.240
for like the big emotional speech,

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:33.800
but be as honest as possible
to who Rob Bilott was,

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:36.950
that's challenging and that
takes a lot of discipline,

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:40.816
and it takes some bit of courage as well.

00:04:40.816 --> 00:04:43.360
So, it was hard for me.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:45.290
- You want to take
everything that you know,

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:48.380
and turn it against an
iconic American company

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.640
like an informant, isn't that right?

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:51.473
- Yes.

00:04:51.473 --> 00:04:54.180
I'm inspired by David and Goliath stories

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:58.170
because we all sort of relate to them,

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:00.690
we all understand those dynamics so well,

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:04.600
and we oddly identify with the David.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:07.780
We understand that we're
living under an oppressive

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:09.660
kind of system at times,

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:13.010
and we always want the
little guy to be able to beat

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:16.083
this monster, this giant.

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:19.880
I just think it's great storytelling

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:24.170
and it's a storytelling
that we really understand

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:27.080
somewhere deep inside of
us in a really simple way.

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:29.310
The system is rigged.

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:31.690
They want us to think it'll protect us.

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:34.060
We protect us, we do.

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:37.218
(dramatic music)

00:05:37.218 --> 00:05:40.718
(ignition switch dinging)

